# Caravans and bandits, in any town (Docs/Design/30-towns-and-talk.md): wagons in and out, the road between, and the
# camps that prey on it. Blanks: {other} (the other town; for a camp, the place it lies). Alternatives run in parallel.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Caravans arrived

scene em_cv_in_bells
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
a : Hear the harness bells? The #topic.where# wagons are in. | The caravan from #topic.where# rolled in at first light. | Wagons from #topic.where#, all of them, not one lost.
b : I heard them from my bed. | Before the gate was even open? | Not one? That's a first this season.
a : Half the street did. | They camped at the wall all night. | Bright One be thanked.
b?[merchant] : I'll be at the yard before the dust settles. First pick is everything.
b?[child] : Can we go and see the horses? Please?

scene em_cv_in_news
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
a : The carters from #topic.where# are full of stories. | What news came in with the #topic.where# caravan? | The #topic.where# drivers say the road's dry the whole way.
b : Stories and dust, the pair of them. | Nothing good, I'll wager. | Dry road, fat purses.
a : They say a bridge washed out near #topic.where#. | You'd lose. They say harvest's good down there. | For once.
b : Then the next one will be late. | Good for them. | Don't jinx it.

scene em_cv_in_unloading
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived here.place=market|street|gate|work
a : Took six of us to unload the #topic.where# wagons. | My back's broken from lifting #topic.where# crates. | They're stacking #topic.where# barrels in the square.
b : Good pay, though? | Your back's always broken. | Barrels of what?
a : Two pennies each. Fair. | It is now. | Nobody's saying. Smells like vinegar.
b?[laborer] : Six of us and one of them watching. As usual.
b?[carter] : Stack them careful. Half of those are wine.

scene em_cv_in_relief
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
a : My brother drives for the caravan. He's home safe from #topic.where#. | I didn't sleep till the #topic.where# wagons came in. | Every time the caravan's late I think the worst.
b : Thank the Bright One. | Was someone you love with it? | And every time it comes, you breathe again.
a : I lit a candle for him. | My brother. | Every time.
b?[spouses] : Come here. You can stop worrying now.

scene em_cv_in_goods
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
a : Cloth from #topic.where#. Proper dyed cloth. | They brought salt fish from #topic.where#. A whole wagon. | The #topic.where# caravan brought pots. Copper pots!
b : About time. My coat's in rags. | Salt fish for a month, then. | Copper! Who can afford copper?
a : You'll look like a noble. | And thirsty with it. | Nobody. They'll sit there all season.
b?[merchant] : And the prices on it would make you weep.
b?[cook] : I'll take two of whatever's fresh.

scene em_cv_in_strangers
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
a : Lots of new faces with the #topic.where# wagons. | One of the #topic.where# carters looked at me like he knew me. | The caravan guards from #topic.where# are a rough lot.
b : Strangers in town. Lock your door. | Do you know him? | Rough is what you want on the road.
a : I always do. | Never seen him in my life. | And not in my tavern.
b?[guard] : We'll keep an eye on them. Carters drink, carters fight.

scene em_cv_in_tavern
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived here.place=tavern
a : The #topic.where# carters have taken every bench. | They're singing #topic.where# songs. Badly. | One of the #topic.where# drivers bought a round for the whole room.
b : We'll stand, then. | Is there any other way? | A round? Where is he?
a : We'll stand and glare. | Not in this tavern. | Asleep under the table.
b?[innkeeper] : Caravan nights pay the winter rent.

scene em_cv_in_letters
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
a : Any letters with the #topic.where# caravan? | My cousin in #topic.where# sent a parcel with the carters. | I'm waiting on word from #topic.where#.
b : Ask the head carter. | What's in it? | Did it come?
a : I will. He owes me a drink. | Socks. She always sends socks. | Not yet.
b?[official] : The post sack's at the hall. Names are read at noon.

scene em_cv_in_brief
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived
weight 0.6
a : Caravan's in from #topic.where#. | The #topic.where# wagons came. | Saw the carters from #topic.where# at the gate.
b : Good. | At last. | Busy day, then.
a : Very good. | At last. | Very.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Caravans left

scene em_cv_out_dawn
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left
a : The caravan left for #topic.where# before dawn. | Wagons rolled out to #topic.where# this morning. | Heard the carts go at first light. Off to #topic.where#.
b : Safe roads to them. | Without me, thankfully. | My mug rattled on the shelf.
a : Safe roads. | You'd hate it. | Mine too.
b?[priest] : May the Bright One walk ahead of them.

scene em_cv_out_sent_goods
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left
a : I sent my whole season's work with the #topic.where# caravan. | Half my stock is on that road to #topic.where#. | If the #topic.where# wagons don't make it, I'm ruined.
b : Then pray for clear skies. | Brave of you. | They'll make it.
a : I am, all day. | Or stupid. | They'd better.
b?[merchant] : Never put it all on one cart. Never.

scene em_cv_out_missing
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left
a : My son went with the caravan to #topic.where#. | First time my daughter's driven the #topic.where# road. | My mate signed on as a guard for the trip to #topic.where#.
b : He'll come back with tales. | She'll be fine. The old drivers look out for the young. | Good coin, guarding.
a : And with fleas. | I hope so. | Dangerous coin.
b?[old] : I drove that road forty years. The fear passes. Mostly.

scene em_cv_out_quiet
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left
a : Town's quiet now the caravan's gone. | No carters, no fights, no singing. | The yard's empty. Only straw and dung.
b : Peace at last. | I'll miss the singing. | And flies.
a : For a week. | You won't. | And flies.
b?[innkeeper] : Quiet and poor, now.

scene em_cv_out_ask
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left
a : Did the caravan take anything of yours to #topic.where#? | When's the next one going to #topic.where#? | You should have gone with the caravan to #topic.where#.
b : Only a letter. | Not till next season. | Me? I'd be sick the whole way.
a : Love letter? | That long? | You're sick on a rope swing.
b : Mind your business. | That long. | Exactly.

scene em_cv_out_brief
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left
weight 0.6
a : Caravan's gone to #topic.where#. | Wagons left for #topic.where#. | The carters went off to #topic.where#.
b : Safe journey to them. | Already? | Good riddance.
a : And home again. | Already. | Ha.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Caravans raided

scene em_cv_raided_shock
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
a : They hit the #topic.where# caravan. Bandits. | Did you hear what happened to the wagons from #topic.where#? | The #topic.where# caravan never made it. Raided.
b : Was anyone hurt? | Tell me nobody died. | Bright One. Again?
a : Two carters, they say. Bad wounds. | Nobody died. This time. | Again.
b?[guard] : Then the captain will want riders on that road by tonight.

scene em_cv_raided_goods_lost
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
a : My order was on the #topic.where# wagons. Gone. | Everything I'd paid for, gone to robbers. | They took the grain from the #topic.where# caravan and burned the rest.
b : Can you get your coin back? | All of it? | Burned? Why burn it?
a : From bandits? | Every penny. | So we'd go hungry.
b?[merchant] : I lost three crates myself. Nobody's covering it.

scene em_cv_raided_anger
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
a : Where was the guard when the #topic.where# caravan was hit? | What do we pay the watch for? | Somebody should hunt those bandits down.
b : Asleep, I expect. | Nothing useful. | Volunteering?
a : Typical. | Exactly. | Maybe I am.
b?[guard] : We're spread thin. Shout at the council, not at me.
b?[soldier] : Give us horses and a free day and we'd finish them.

scene em_cv_raided_fear
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
a : I'll not travel to #topic.where# till the road's cleared. | Nobody's driving to #topic.where# after that raid. | They'll attack the next one too. You watch.
b : Neither would I. | Then we'll go without. | Don't say that.
a : Sensible. | Go without what? Everything comes that way. | Someone has to.
b?[carter] : I'll drive. Someone has to. But I want double.

scene em_cv_raided_prices
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
a : Prices will jump now the #topic.where# wagons are lost. | Buy what you need today. Tomorrow it'll cost more. | Every raid comes out of our bread.
b : I know. I'm buying now. | Now you tell me. | Out of everyone's bread.
a : Quick about it. | I told you yesterday. | Except the bandits'.
b?[merchant] : I've already changed my slate.

scene em_cv_raided_survivor
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
a : One of the #topic.where# carters walked in alone. Covered in mud. | They brought the wounded in on a hay cart. | A driver from the #topic.where# caravan is at the healer's. Still shaking.
b : What did he say? | Who's tending them? | Shaking? I'd never stop.
a : Twenty of them, he said. Masked. | The healer and anyone with clean cloth. | Nor would I.
b?[healer] : He'll live. His nerves might not.

scene em_cv_raided_brief
when topic=caravan topic.kind=raided
weight 0.6
a : The #topic.where# caravan got hit. | Bandits on the #topic.where# road. | Wagons raided. From #topic.where#.
b : Again? | Bad. | No.
a : Again. | Very bad. | Yes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Bandits on the roads

scene em_bd_raided_where
when topic=bandits topic.kind=raided
a : Bandits on the road to #topic.where#. Wagons burned. | Did you hear? The #topic.where# road's not safe. | They hit carts bound for #topic.where#, bold as anything.
b : In daylight? | Since when? | Bold or desperate.
a : At noon, they say. | Since last week. | Both, I think.
b?[old] : The road's been bad before. It gets cleared. It gets bad again.

scene em_bd_raided_who
when topic=bandits topic.kind=raided
a : Who are these bandits, anyway? | They say the robbers are deserters from some army. | My cousin swears the bandits are farmers gone hungry.
b : Nobody knows. That's the fear of it. | Could be. | Hungry farmers don't burn wagons.
a : That's the fear of it. | Could be. | Angry ones might.
b?[soldier] : Deserters fight better than that. These are amateurs who got lucky.

scene em_bd_raided_travel
when topic=bandits topic.kind=raided
a : I was meant to go to #topic.where# tomorrow. | My sister's coming from #topic.where#. I'm sick with worry. | No one's going to #topic.where# unless they're mad or armed.
b : Wait a few days. | Send word. Tell her to wait. | Or both.
a : I'll have to. | It'll never reach her in time. | Usually both.

scene em_bd_raided_guards
when topic=bandits topic.kind=raided b.job=guard|soldier
a : Are you going after the bandits? | Will the watch ride out against them? | You're a guard. Do something about the road to #topic.where#.
b : If the captain says so. I'm not riding out alone. | When we have the riders. | I'm a guard. Not an army.
a : Then tell the captain. | When's that? | Then find an army.

scene em_bd_raided_insurance
when topic=bandits topic.kind=raided a.role=merchant
a : I'm hiring guards for my next cart. | I'll pay double for an escort to #topic.where# now. | Every raid costs me a guard's wage, one way or another.
b : Guards cost money. | Double? | Same coin, different robber.
a : So does losing the cart. | And worth it. | Ha. Truer than you know.

scene em_bd_raided_brief
when topic=bandits topic.kind=raided
weight 0.6
a : Bandits again. | The #topic.where# road's bad. | More raids.
b : Always bandits. | Avoid it. | Of course.
a : Always. | I will. | Of course.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Bandit camps

scene em_bd_camp_smoke
when topic=bandits topic.kind=camp
a : There's smoke up by #topic.where#. Camp fires. | Someone saw bandits camped near #topic.where#. | Don't go near #topic.where#. There's a camp.
b : Bandits or charcoal burners? | How many? | I wasn't planning to.
a : Charcoal burners don't post sentries. | Enough to worry about. | Good.

scene em_bd_camp_scared
when topic=bandits topic.kind=camp
a : I won't walk past #topic.where# even in daylight now. | My pups aren't allowed beyond the gate while that camp's at #topic.where#. | They watch the road from #topic.where#. You can feel it.
b : Wise. | Quite right. | I've felt it. Like eyes on your neck.
a : Or cowardly. | They hate it. | That's it exactly.
b?[guard] : Stay inside the walls after dark and you've nothing to fear.

scene em_bd_camp_rumour
when topic=bandits topic.kind=camp
a : They say the camp at #topic.where# has a leader with one ear. | Somebody said the bandits at #topic.where# pay for their bread with stolen silver. | They say there's a Gifted wolf in that camp.
b : One ear and no mercy, I suppose. | Someone's selling them bread, then. | Gifted? Probably lights their fires.
a : That's what they say. | Someone in this town. | Probably.
b?[scholar] : Most Gifts are small. A camp fire is about right.

scene em_bd_camp_clear
when topic=bandits topic.kind=camp
a : Someone should clear out that camp at #topic.where#. | The watch could finish them in a night. | Why hasn't anyone driven them off from #topic.where#?
b : Someone with spears. | The watch couldn't find their own feet. | Nobody wants to go.
a : Volunteer, then. | Unkind. Probably true. | Nobody ever does.
b?[guard] : The watch guards the town. The country's not ours.
b?[soldier] : Send a proper company, not a watch. They'd scatter by morning.

scene em_bd_camp_sightings
when topic=bandits topic.kind=camp here.place=gate|field|wild|street
a : Saw shapes moving on the ridge toward #topic.where#. | Fresh tracks coming down from #topic.where#. | Smelled smoke and old meat downwind of #topic.where#.
b : Tell the watch. | Fresh? How fresh? | That's them.
a : I did. They wrote it down. | This morning's. | That's them.
b?[herder] : They took two sheep from my flock last night.
b?[woodcutter] : I've stopped cutting in that wood.

scene em_bd_camp_brief
when topic=bandits topic.kind=camp
weight 0.6
a : That camp's still there. | Bandits at #topic.where#. | Smoke from #topic.where# again.
b : I know. | Still? | Stay away.
a : I know. | Still. | I will.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Roads, by time and weather

scene em_cv_in_rain
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived here.weather=rain|storm
a : The #topic.where# wagons came in through the mud. | Carters from #topic.where# soaked to the bone. | Wheels caked to the axles, those #topic.where# carts.
b : Brave of them. | They'll be in the tavern drying out. | It'll take a day to scrape them.
a : Or desperate for coin. | And drinking. | Two days.

scene em_cv_in_snow
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived here.weather=snow
a : A caravan from #topic.where#, through all this snow. | The #topic.where# carters had to dig out twice, they said. | Snow on every crate from #topic.where#.
b : Mad wolves. | Only twice? | Keeps it fresh.
a : Rich mad wolves. | They were lucky. | True enough.

scene em_cv_in_night
when topic=caravan topic.kind=arrived here.time=dusk|night
a : The #topic.where# caravan came in late. Lanterns all down the road. | Wagons from #topic.where# came rolling in after dark. | They opened the gate at night for the #topic.where# wagons.
b : Pretty sight, the lanterns. | Lucky they found the road. | Rules are rules, except when they aren't.
a : Like a festival. | Lucky indeed. | Exactly.
b?[guard] : We don't like it. But we don't leave them outside either.

scene em_cv_out_rain
when topic=caravan topic.kind=left here.weather=rain|storm|fog
a : Wagons left for #topic.where# in this weather. | They'll be stuck by noon. | Caravan went off to #topic.where# in a fog thick as porridge.
b : They'll be stuck by noon. | Probably. | Hope the lead wolf has a good nose.
a : Probably. | They'll manage. | The best in town.

scene em_bd_night_fear
when topic=bandits here.time=dusk|night
a : I lock my door twice now, with the bandits about. | Every noise at night sounds like a raid. | Bandits come at night, they say. In the dark.
b : Can't blame you. | It's only the wind. | And by day, from what I hear.
a : Three times, some nights. | Is it? | Then I'll never sleep.
